\begin{abstract}
Hakobyan and Mkrtchyan conjectured that every cubic graph whose edge set can
be covered by at most four perfect matchings admits a $P_{12}$-coloring,
where $P_{12}$ is obtained from the Petersen graph by replacing one vertex
with a triangle. We show that the two $18$-vertex Blanu\v{s}a snarks are
counterexamples. Explicit perfect-matching covers and an exact finite
enumeration establish that each graph has perfect-matching index four and
admits no $P_{12}$-coloring. Both graphs nevertheless admit Petersen
colorings. Hence $P_{12}$-colorability is strictly stronger than
Petersen-colorability, even among cubic graphs of perfect-matching index at
most four.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.}
Cubic graph; $H$-coloring; Petersen coloring; perfect matching; snark.\\
\textbf{MSC 2020.} 05C15, 05C70.

\section{The problem and the counterexample}

All graphs are finite and loopless; parallel edges are allowed, although the
graphs below are simple. For a vertex $v$ of a graph $G$, let
$\starat{G}{v}$ denote the set of edges incident with $v$. If $G$ and $H$
are cubic graphs, an \emph{$H$-coloring} of $G$ is a map
\[
    f:E(G)\longrightarrow E(H)
\]
such that, for every $v\in V(G)$, there is a vertex $x\in V(H)$ with
\[
    f\bigl(\starat{G}{v}\bigr)=\starat{H}{x}.
\]
We write $H\prec G$ when such a coloring exists. This relation is
transitive \cite{HakobyanMkrtchyan2019}.

Let $k(G)$ be the minimum number of perfect matchings whose union is $E(G)$.
Let $\Pten$ be the Petersen graph, and let $\Ptwelve$ be the cubic graph
obtained from $\Pten$ by replacing one vertex with a triangle. Hakobyan and
Mkrtchyan formulated the following $P_{12}$-conjecture
\cite{HakobyanMkrtchyan2019}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:conjecture}
    k(G)\leq 4 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \Ptwelve\prec G
    \qquad\text{for every cubic graph }G.
\end{equation}

Let $\Bone$ and $\Btwo$ denote the two Blanu\v{s}a snarks. Exact labeled
edge sets are given in Appendix~\ref{app:data}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
For each $i\in\{1,2\}$,
\[
    k(B_i)=4,\qquad \Pten\prec B_i,\qquad\text{and}\qquad
    \Ptwelve\not\prec B_i.
\]
Consequently, the $P_{12}$-conjecture is false.
\end{theorem}

The graph $\Ptwelve$ admits a Petersen coloring, that is,
$\Pten\prec\Ptwelve$ \cite{HakobyanMkrtchyan2019}. By transitivity,
$\Ptwelve\prec G$ therefore implies $\Pten\prec G$. Theorem~\ref{thm:main}
shows that the implication is strict even after restricting to cubic graphs
with $k(G)\leq4$.

\section{Why the graphs are counterexamples}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}]
For each $i\in\{1,2\}$, the four perfect matchings displayed in
Appendix~\ref{app:data} cover $E(B_i)$, and hence $k(B_i)\leq4$.

The remaining statements are verified by exhaustive finite enumeration. A
proper $3$-edge-coloring can be represented by choosing, at every source
vertex, one of the six bijections from its three incident edges to
$\{1,2,3\}$. More generally, for a cubic target graph $H$, a local state at
$v\in V(G)$ consists of a vertex $x\in V(H)$ and a bijection
\[
    \starat{G}{v}\longrightarrow\starat{H}{x}.
\]
There are $6|V(H)|$ such states at each source vertex. States at adjacent
vertices are compatible exactly when they assign the same label in the
$3$-edge-coloring case, or the same edge of $H$ in the $H$-coloring case,
to their common source edge. Thus global colorings are in bijection with
compatible choices of one local state at every source vertex.

Enumerating all compatible assignments gives
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
source graph & $3$-edge-colorings & $\Pten$-colorings & $\Ptwelve$-colorings\\
\midrule
$\Bone$ & 0 & 480 & 0\\
$\Btwo$ & 0 & 240 & 0\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The positive middle column proves $\Pten\prec B_i$, and the zero final
column proves $\Ptwelve\not\prec B_i$.

It remains only to justify equality in $k(B_i)\leq4$. If three perfect
matchings covered a cubic graph $G$, then their total number of edge
occurrences would be
\[
    3\frac{|V(G)|}{2}=|E(G)|.
\]
They would therefore be pairwise disjoint and would form a proper
$3$-edge-coloring. Since the first numerical column is zero, no such cover
exists. Hence $k(B_i)=4$.

The accompanying program \texttt{blanusa\_p12\_verify.py} contains the
labeled graph data, verifies the displayed perfect-matching covers,
constructs $\Pten$ and $\Ptwelve$ from their definitions, and performs all
three enumerations using only the Python standard library.
\end{proof}

\section{Origin and related literature}

The examples arise naturally from the conjecture. If a cubic graph $G$ is
$3$-edge-colorable, then it admits an $H$-coloring for every cubic graph
$H$: map the three color classes to the three edges incident with a fixed
vertex of $H$. Any counterexample to~\eqref{eq:conjecture} must therefore be
non-$3$-edge-colorable. The Petersen graph is the first natural test case,
but its perfect-matching index is five and it does not satisfy the
hypothesis \cite{FouquetVanherpe2009}. The two Blanu\v{s}a snarks,
introduced in 1946 \cite{Blanusa1946}, are the next classical small snarks
and have perfect-matching index four, making them natural candidates.

The perfect-matching-index statement for the Blanu\v{s}a snarks was already
established by Fouquet and Vanherpe \cite{FouquetVanherpe2009}.
H\"agglund and Steffen proved that all generalized Blanu\v{s}a snarks of
both types admit Petersen colorings \cite{HagglundSteffen2014}. The
contribution of the present note is the non-$P_{12}$-colorability of the two
classical members and the resulting separation between $P_{12}$- and
Petersen-colorability under the condition $k(G)\leq4$.

Putman recently constructed a $112$-vertex bridgeless cubic graph with no
Petersen coloring \cite{Putman2026}. Since $\Pten\prec\Ptwelve$, that graph
also has no $P_{12}$-coloring. Putman's preprint does not determine its
perfect-matching index, so this fact alone does not place the graph under
the hypothesis of~\eqref{eq:conjecture}. The Blanu\v{s}a examples have a
different profile: they satisfy $k(G)=4$ and remain Petersen-colorable.

